{
  "slug": "artist:fela-kuti",
  "name": "Fela Kuti",
  "editorial_article": "Born in 1938 in Abeokuta, Nigeria, Fela Kuti pioneered the Afrobeat genre by fusing traditional Yoruba chants with American jazz, funk, and highlife. His ensemble, Africa 70, featured the influential drummer Tony Allen, whose technical percussion style became foundational to the genre's complex rhythms. Following a 1969 trip to Los Angeles where he encountered the Black Panther Party through Sandra Akanke Isidore, Kuti shifted his focus toward radical political critique. This transformation manifested in the 1977 album Zombie, a sharp satirical attack on the Nigerian military that prompted a government raid on his Kalakuta Republic commune. Kuti continued his protest through albums like 1989's Beasts of No Nation, using his music to challenge corruption and authoritarianism across Africa.",
